package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

// CAPABILITY. Grant name: "usage-read".
// Consent: "see how much of your AI coding tools' limits you have used".

/// How much of an AI coding tool's rate limit has been spent.
///
/// A share of a window and how long until it resets: "31% of five hours, back
/// in 1h 12m". Nothing else.
///
/// # The host asks, the plugin is told
///
/// The figures come from the service, and asking for them needs the tool's own
/// sign-in — an OAuth token sitting in that tool's config directory. That token
/// is enormous authority: it can spend the account it belongs to. So the host
/// holds it, makes the request, and hands across the two numbers that came
/// back.
///
/// The shape of this interface is the guarantee. There is no field here that
/// could carry a credential, a path, a transcript or a project name, so a guest
/// cannot ask for one and a host cannot leak one through an oversight. The
/// consent line says "see how much of your AI tool's limit you have used",
/// because that is the whole of what is on offer.
///
/// This is the same shape as `cap-net-fetch`'s credential: the sensitive thing
/// stays host-side and the guest is given the one derived fact it needs.
///
/// # Ages, not timestamps
///
/// There is no wall clock in this world — see `cap-clock`, which says why: it
/// is a fingerprinting and correlation surface for no benefit. A reset is
/// therefore reported as a duration rather than as a moment.
///
/// That is not a workaround, it is the better shape for the question. "When
/// does my window reset" is answered by "in an hour", and a guest that knew the
/// wall time could correlate this machine's activity with anything else it ever
/// sees. One that knows "1h 12m" draws the same countdown and nothing else.
///
/// # What this used to be, and why it is not
///
/// It once reported token counts summed out of the tool's own session files,
/// hour by hour. That meant the host read transcripts — every prompt, every
/// reply, every path — to add up four integers, and it meant a plugin could ask
/// for a four-week window and block on six hundred megabytes of reading.
///
/// Nothing ever asked for those counts. A capability that reads the most
/// sensitive thing on the machine had better be earning it, and this one was
/// not, so it was removed rather than kept against a future that had not
/// arrived. Adding it back later is additive — a new function on this
/// interface, which no published component imports and therefore none breaks
/// over.
interface usage-read {
  use types.{host-error};

  /// Which tool's records to read, by name.
  ///
  /// A string rather than a closed enum, and the reason is the ecosystem rather
  /// than this interface. An enum would mean that supporting a second AI tool
  /// required editing this file — a versioned interface every published plugin
  /// is compiled against — so every developer who wanted to read a tool the
  /// host had not blessed would need the host's author to ship a release first.
  /// That is a plugin system whose ceiling is however much time its maintainer
  /// has, which is the failure this whole capability model exists to avoid.
  ///
  /// **A name is not a path.** The host maps a name it recognises onto a
  /// location it chose; a name it does not recognise is `host-error`, not a
  /// lookup. So a guest still cannot say where to read, which is the property
  /// the enum was protecting — the enum was simply the wrong mechanism for it,
  /// because it put the *list* in the ABI instead of in the host.
  ///
  /// Lowercase, dashes, matching the manifest's other identifiers.
  /// `claude-code` is the one the host knows today.
  type tool = string;

  record window {
    /// How much of the window is used, `[0, 1]`.
    used: f32,
    /// Minutes until it resets.
    ///
    /// Minutes rather than the epoch second the tool publishes, because this
    /// world has no wall clock and does not want one — see `cap-clock`. A
    /// countdown is what a reading shows anyway.
    resets-in-minutes: u32,
  }

  /// What a tool says about its own limits.
  record limits {
    /// The short window. Five hours, for Claude Code.
    session: window,
    /// The long one. Seven days, for Claude Code.
    week: window,
    /// Whether the tool is currently allowed to make requests.
    ///
    /// A window at 100% and a window that is actually blocked are different
    /// states, and a reading that could not tell them apart would show a full
    /// bar for both.
    blocked: bool,

    /// How many minutes ago these figures were measured.
    ///
    /// The host does not generate them — it reads what the tool last wrote —
    /// and a tool only writes while it is running. So a figure can be an hour
    /// old on a machine where nothing is wrong, and a reading that showed it
    /// without saying so would be presenting stale data as live.
    ///
    /// Zero means the host has just seen it change. A guest should show this
    /// whenever it is more than a couple of minutes.
    measured-minutes-ago: u32,
  }

  /// The tool's own rate-limit figures.
  ///
  /// Fails when there is nothing to ask with. Not every tool is signed in on
  /// this machine, and one signed in with an API key rather than a subscription
  /// has no rate-limit windows to report.
  quota: func(recorded-by: tool) -> result<limits, host-error>;

}
